# macOS AppleDouble sidecars. ~6,100 of these accumulate in this tree from
# copying across non-native filesystems. They are metadata, never source, and
# they inflate every file count and line count taken over the tree. The owner
# asked on 2026-08-03 that they be set aside rather than deleted, so they are
# ignored here rather than removed.
._*
**/._*

# Test and conformance evidence: ~82 MB of json, txt, pcap and nftables dumps
# regenerated by every run. Kept out of the sync so the repository stays a
# record of source, not of output.
#
# DO NOT `git add -f` a capture bundle. This comment used to end with exactly
# that invitation -- "commit a specific evidence bundle by path with `git add
# -f` when one needs to be preserved as an artifact" -- and preserving a bundle
# is a real need, so the invitation was always going to be taken. It is the
# wrong mechanism. When a bundle must outlive the working tree: record its
# per-file sha256 manifest (the failed-batch-14/15 pattern), move the bytes
# BYTE-EXACT into the no-remote archive repository (`wins/docs`, which holds
# the node map already and cannot push anywhere), and cite it from handoff
# docs by manifest hash plus placeholder-scrubbed excerpts. Raw bytes in the
# archive; placeholders in any repository that has a remote.
#
# UNANCHORED ON PURPOSE -- this rule was `/winn_protocol_experimental/evidence/`
# until 2026-08-07. A leading slash anchors to the repository root, and on
# 2026-08-03 commit d93e80d wrote a DOUBLED path:
#
#     winn_protocol_experimental/winn_protocol_experimental/evidence/...
#
# The anchor did not match it, so 108 files entered the index and were pushed
# to GitHub the same day -- three of them real `.pcap` binaries, which the
# pre-push gate passed WITHOUT READING because it sniffed binary blobs and
# silently skipped them.
#
# Do not read that last sentence as history. It was true of the gate as
# shipped, and it was still true this morning. It stopped being true in the
# SAME change that rewrote this rule (2026-08-07): `tools/ipscrub/ipscrub.py`
# now raises a blocking `binary-evidence` finding instead of skipping, for any
# binary blob it cannot read on an evidence path. If you are reading this
# comment to decide whether the hole is closed, do not trust the comment --
# run `python3 tools/ipscrub/ipscrub.py scan` and look for
# "Unscannable binary on an evidence path". A comment that claims a fix is
# worse than a known gap, because the next person reads it and stops looking.
#
# Nobody decided to publish those captures; a path shape decided it. So the
# rule is now written so that no path shape can step around it.
winn_protocol_experimental/**/evidence/

# ...with two named exceptions, because the rule above is deliberately blunt
# and this subtree contains directories called `evidence` that are SOURCE, not
# capture output. Both are tracked today; without these lines any NEW file
# added beside them would be silently ignored, which is the same class of
# silent failure this block exists to fix.
#
# `Evidence/` is spelled with a capital E, and on this checkout
# core.ignorecase=true (measured, not assumed -- macOS), so the blunt rule
# reaches it here and would NOT reach it on Linux CI. Listing both spellings is
# what makes the two platforms agree.
!winn_protocol_experimental/spikes/ios_maximum_privacy/Evidence/
!winn_protocol_experimental/spikes/ios_maximum_privacy/stagebphysicalverify/testdata/canonical/evidence/

# Packet captures, anywhere in the tree. `.pcap` is the one file class here
# that CANNOT be placeholder-scrubbed: the addresses are in IP headers with
# checksums over them, so "redacting" a pcap manufactures a new file rather
# than editing one. Keep-as-is, relocate, or destroy are the only honest
# options -- which makes "never commit it" the only control that actually
# works. Three were committed anyway, through the doubled path above.
*.pcap
*.pcapng

# Sibling evidence drops. Lanes also write dated capture directories NEXT TO
# the evidence tree instead of inside it -- `.e8_linux_evidence_20260802/` is
# one, and it was untracked and unignored until this line. Matched by shape,
# not by instance, so the next one is covered on the day it is created.
**/*_evidence_[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]/
**/.*_evidence_*/

# Built binaries and packaged Flutter assets. 71 MB of build output that was
# tracked; rebuild it rather than clone it.
/release/

# Staged Winn Protocol engine binaries (~19 MB per rebuild).
#
# DERIVED ARTIFACT, NOT A VENDORED DEPENDENCY -- that distinction is the whole
# decision. libxray.so IS tracked, and correctly so: Xray's source is not in
# this repository, so the binary is the only copy and committing it is what
# makes the build reproducible. libwinnengine.so is built from
# winn_protocol_experimental/, which IS in this repository, by
# android/tools/prepare_android_winn_assets.sh --stage in about a minute given
# Go and the NDK.
#
# Committing a build output of in-repo source creates a staleness trap, and that
# trap has already fired on this project: on 2026-08-04 the checked-in
# libwinn-datapath-{darwin,ios}-arm64.a archives were built at 07:39, before
# s1..s4 were added to datapath.AWGHeaderTypes at 13:56. Their cgo header was
# byte-identical, so verify_winn_abi_conformance.sh PASSED while WinnTunnelCreate
# refused every config with `json: unknown field "s1"`. Signature checking
# structurally cannot catch that, because datapath.Config uses
# DisallowUnknownFields. A tracked binary that silently disagrees with the source
# beside it is worse than no binary.
#
# Also: each rebuild is a new ~19 MB blob in history forever. This repository
# was 1.5 GB and had to be rewritten to remove 190 MB of images.
#
# These live in the DEBUG source set on purpose -- item D2 defers the release
# variant while 1.0.14+29 is in store review. Do not move them to src/main.
/android/app/src/debug/jniLibs/**/libwinnengine.so

# Winn conformance build output -- the SAME rule as the block above, applied to
# the artifacts that incident was actually discovered in.
#
# These 23 files were tracked: five datapath c-archives with their cgo headers,
# an iOS client binding, and nine winn-clientinfo binaries across android/darwin/
# linux/windows on both amd64 and arm64. All of them are output of
# tools/build_winn_protocol_engine.sh, which builds from
# winn_protocol_experimental/ -- in this repository -- so every argument above
# applies unchanged: derived artifact, not a vendored dependency.
#
# The staleness cited above was found HERE. These were the archives built before
# s1..s4 joined datapath.AWGHeaderTypes, that passed every ABI check because
# their cgo header was byte-identical while refusing every current config.
#
# Nothing needs them checked in. tools/winn_conformance_verify.py always built
# its own -- build_matrix() invokes the build script for every target and reports
# a missing NDK or Xcode as UNVERIFIED with real stderr. The two scripts that
# merely EXPECTED them, tools/verify_winn_abi_conformance.sh and
# tools/build_apple_engine_wrappers.sh, now build what they are missing, after
# the model tools/verify_apple_winn_seam.sh already set: build what you verify,
# so what you verify cannot be older than what it is verified against. No CI
# workflow references any of it.
/tools/winn_conformance_build_out/

# The whole directory, one level down from the repository root's own
# tools/ -- not just the winn_conformance_build_out/ subdirectory previously
# named here. Widened to the parent on 2026-08-09 after a second, independent
# incident confirmed the same conclusion the first one reached: nothing
# genuinely source ever lives at winn_protocol_experimental/tools/, so the
# directory itself is the thing worth ignoring, not one accident shape under
# it.
#
# Incident 1 (found 2026-08-06, committed by d93e80d): this path held 23
# TRACKED files -- winn_conformance_verify.py's BUILD_OUT_DIR
# (ROOT/"tools"/"winn_conformance_build_out") resolved against
# winn_protocol_experimental/ instead of the repository root. Five of its
# seven binaries were already modified in the working tree against their
# committed copies -- rebuilt 2026-08-04 06:39 -- so tracking them was
# generating permanent dirty-tree noise on top of everything in the block
# above. Untracked via git rm --cached at the time.
#
# Incident 2 (found 2026-08-09): a RELATIVE OUTPUT_ARG bug in
# tools/build_winn_protocol_engine.sh -- the script `cd`s to
# winn_protocol_experimental/ (MODULE_DIR) to run `go build`, so a caller
# passing a relative output path like
# "tools/winn_conformance_build_out/libwinn-datapath-darwin-arm64.a.rebuilt"
# got it written under winn_protocol_experimental/tools/... while every
# other check in the script read the SAME relative path from the caller's
# own working directory instead, validated whatever stale file already sat
# there, and reported success. Orphans dated 2026-08-08 15:15
# (libwinn-datapath-darwin-arm64.a.h and .a.rebuilt -- the .h because go's
# c-archive header naming replaces the LAST extension, so a ".a.rebuilt"
# output produced a ".a.h" header) were that build; they were left in place
# as evidence and have since been deleted by hand (untracked, so no `git rm`
# was needed). The script itself is fixed now -- OUTPUT_ARG is absolutised
# before use, and every build lands in a private staging directory and is
# identity-checked by digest before anything is validated or installed --
# but the fix lives in the script, not in what gets ignored here, so it does
# not by itself stop a future relative path (a different filename, a
# different caller) from spilling somewhere else directly under
# winn_protocol_experimental/tools/ that the old, narrower rule would have
# missed.
#
# Checked before widening (2026-08-09): no script, workflow, build file, or
# Go import anywhere in the repository treats winn_protocol_experimental/tools/
# as a real source location -- the module's actual build tooling
# (build_winn_protocol_engine.sh, winn_conformance_verify.py, the verify_*.sh
# scripts) lives under the repository root's own tools/, not inside the Go
# module. The directory held nothing but the two incidents above both times
# it was inspected.
/winn_protocol_experimental/tools/

# Backups taken by the iOS winn/3 dev-session safety lane (2026-08-06).
.ios_winn3_backups_20260806/

# Run evidence for winn/3 capability decisions is committed under
# .winn_platform_progress/evidence/ -- but *.log above (line 3) silently
# swallowed anything dropped there with a .log extension, and git gives no
# warning when that happens; it just omits the file from `git status`. The
# two Android device-check logs that back kWinnHostCapabilities['android']
# in lib/winn_protocol_dispatch.dart (devicecheck_20260804T*.log) sat
# untracked in this directory for exactly this reason. *.log stays global --
# this negates it for this ONE directory only, non-recursively, so build
# logs everywhere else stay ignored exactly as before. Confirmed with
# `git check-ignore -v` that neither .winn_platform_progress/ nor
# .winn_platform_progress/evidence/ is excluded as a directory anywhere in
# this file, which is what would have silently defeated a negation like this
# one (git does not descend into an already-ignored directory to evaluate
# per-file re-includes).
!/.winn_platform_progress/evidence/*.log

# Backups taken by the winn/3 evidence-gate + dangling-citation repair lane
# (2026-08-06). Same convention as .ios_winn3_backups_20260806/ above.
.winn3_evidence_gate_backups_20260806/

